  "fixture_kind": "public_exact-list_card_data",
  "rulings": [
    {
      "comment": "This has basic land types, but it isn't a basic land. Things that affect basic lands don't affect it. Things that affect basic land types do.",
      "oracle_id": "22e3cf1d-3559-4ce1-954c-8dc815342979",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "This has the mana abilities associated with both of its basic land types.",
      "oracle_id": "22e3cf1d-3559-4ce1-954c-8dc815342979",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If Alpine Guide can't attack for any reason (such as being tapped or having come under a player's control that turn), then it doesn't attack. If there's a cost associated with having it attack, its controller isn't forced to pay that cost, so it doesn't have to attack in that case either.",
      "oracle_id": "55b12241-2495-45e6-b618-3746d5521291",
      "published_at": "2019-06-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If you control only colorless permanents as the ability resolves (for example, basic lands and face-down permanents), you won\u2019t sacrifice anything.",
      "oracle_id": "6b87a933-b0d2-4120-b4a0-65ab0f3dbe48",
      "published_at": "2014-11-24",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Powerstone Shard\u2019s activated ability is a mana ability. It doesn\u2019t use the stack and can\u2019t be responded to.",
      "oracle_id": "769c45ae-5d49-4815-944e-98790ad4efb0",
      "published_at": "2018-04-27",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Although all the cards in The Brothers' War that create Powerstone tokens create a tapped Powerstone token, entering the battlefield tapped isn't part of the token's definition. Notably, if you create a token that is a copy of a Powerstone token, the token copy won't enter the battlefield tapped.",
      "oracle_id": "78d74d6a-e1aa-4d70-92c3-b5338959d2fb",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Powerstone tokens are a kind of predefined token. Each one has the artifact subtype \"Powerstone\" and the ability \"{T}: Add {C}. This mana can't be spent to cast a nonartifact spell.\"",
      "oracle_id": "78d74d6a-e1aa-4d70-92c3-b5338959d2fb",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "You can use the {C} added by a Powerstone token on anything that isn't a nonartifact spell. This includes paying costs to activate abilities of both artifact and nonartifact permanents, paying ward costs, and so on.",
      "oracle_id": "78d74d6a-e1aa-4d70-92c3-b5338959d2fb",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an attacking creature loses haste, perhaps because Fervor leaves the battlefield after attackers have been declared, it won't be removed from combat.",
      "oracle_id": "8e0cea9c-3110-4728-9378-76849e33bb90",
      "published_at": "2012-07-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "A card with devoid is just colorless. It's not colorless and the colors of mana in its mana cost.",
      "oracle_id": "b2977807-5f46-424a-a6e9-14262f8a723a",
      "published_at": "2015-08-25",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Cards with devoid use frames that are variations of the transparent frame traditionally used for Eldrazi. The top part of the card features some color over a background based on the texture of the hedrons that once imprisoned the Eldrazi. This coloration is intended to aid deckbuilding and game play.",
      "oracle_id": "b2977807-5f46-424a-a6e9-14262f8a723a",
      "published_at": "2015-08-25",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Devoid works in all zones, not just on the battlefield.",
      "oracle_id": "b2977807-5f46-424a-a6e9-14262f8a723a",
      "published_at": "2015-08-25",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a card loses devoid, it will still be colorless. This is because effects that change an object's color (like the one created by devoid) are considered before the object loses devoid.",
      "oracle_id": "b2977807-5f46-424a-a6e9-14262f8a723a",
      "published_at": "2015-08-25",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If the player has no cards in their library when the ingest ability resolves, nothing happens. That player won't lose the game (until they have to draw a card from an empty library).",
      "oracle_id": "b2977807-5f46-424a-a6e9-14262f8a723a",
      "published_at": "2015-08-25",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Other cards and abilities can give a card with devoid color. If that happens, it's just the new color, not that color and colorless.",
      "oracle_id": "b2977807-5f46-424a-a6e9-14262f8a723a",
      "published_at": "2015-08-25",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The card exiled by the ingest ability is exiled face up.",
      "oracle_id": "b2977807-5f46-424a-a6e9-14262f8a723a",
      "published_at": "2015-08-25",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "A player prompted to name a card may name the combined back face, and each player has the right to know that combined back face's characteristics at all times.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an effect moves a melded permanent to a new zone and then affects \"that card,\" it affects both cards.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In the Commander variant, a meld card's color identity is determined only by the mana costs and mana symbols in the rules text of its front face. No symbols or rules text of the permanent it melds into are considered.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Note that the permanent represented by the combined back faces has a color indicator.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "One card in each pair of meld cards has an ability that instructs you to exile the two cards and meld them. If you control more than one object with one of those names, you select one object with that name to exile.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Only two cards belonging to the same meld pair can be melded. Tokens, cards that aren't meld cards, or meld cards that don't form a meld pair can't be melded. If an effect instructs a player to meld cards that can't be melded, those cards remain in exile.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The mana value of a melded permanent is the sum of the mana values of its front faces. A creature that becomes a copy of a melded permanent has only the characteristics of that combined back face, and its mana value is 0.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "When a pair of cards are melded, the result is a single creature that's represented by two cards. If the melded creature dies, both cards are put into your graveyard. As it leaves the battlefield, both of those cards are turned face up again. If the cards are put on the top or bottom of your library, you choose their relative order.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "When two cards are exiled and melded, they each leave the battlefield, then return together as one new object with no relation to either of the objects that left the battlefield. Counters, Auras, Equipment, and other effects that affected those two cards don't affect the melded permanent.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "While a meld card is in any zone other than the battlefield, it has only the characteristics of its front face. The same is true while it's on the battlefield with its front face up.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "While a melded permanent is on the battlefield, it has only the characteristics of its combined back face. Any effects that modify how the new object enters the battlefield will consider only the combined back face.",
      "oracle_id": "c396db03-bf11-4e20-b630-4f9aa8fd78da",
      "published_at": "2022-10-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In a Limited event (usually Booster Draft or Sealed Deck), you can't add basic snow lands to your card pool as you would other basic lands. You can play with basic snow lands only if you open them in your sealed deck or draft them.",
      "oracle_id": "ca9f660b-e07d-4f42-a46e-abd0ca72510c",
      "published_at": "2021-02-05",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Snow is a supertype, not a card type. It has no rules meaning or function by itself, but spells and abilities may refer to it.",
      "oracle_id": "ca9f660b-e07d-4f42-a46e-abd0ca72510c",
      "published_at": "2021-02-05",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Snow isn't a type of mana. If an effect says you may spend mana as though it were any type, you can't pay for {S} using mana that wasn't produced by a snow source.",
      "oracle_id": "ca9f660b-e07d-4f42-a46e-abd0ca72510c",
      "published_at": "2021-02-05",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Some cards have additional effects for each {S} spent to cast them. You can cast these spells even if you don't spend any snow mana to cast them; their additional effects simply won't do anything.",
      "oracle_id": "ca9f660b-e07d-4f42-a46e-abd0ca72510c",
      "published_at": "2021-02-05",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The Kaldheim set doesn't have any cards with mana costs that include {S}, but some previous sets do. If an effect says such a spell costs {1} less to cast, that reduction doesn't apply to any {S} costs. This is also true for activated abilities that include {S} in their activation costs and effects that reduce those costs.",
      "oracle_id": "ca9f660b-e07d-4f42-a46e-abd0ca72510c",
      "published_at": "2021-02-05",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The {S} symbol is a generic mana symbol. It represents a cost that can be paid by one mana that was produced by a snow source. That mana can be any color or colorless.",
      "oracle_id": "ca9f660b-e07d-4f42-a46e-abd0ca72510c",
      "published_at": "2021-02-05",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If Dryad Arbor is changed into another basic land type (such as by Sea's Claim), it continues to be a creature and a Dryad.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2011-09-22",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Although originally printed with a characteristic-defining ability that defined its color, this card now has a color indicator. This color indicator can't be affected by text-changing effects (such as the one created by Crystal Spray), although color-changing effects can still overwrite it.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2013-06-07",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Dryad Arbor is played as a land. It doesn't use the stack, it's not a spell, it can't be responded to, it has no mana cost, and it counts as your land play for the turn.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Due to its color indicator (appearing to the left of its type line), Dryad Arbor is green. Color indicators apply in all zones, not just the battlefield.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Forest is a land type and Dryad is a creature type.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If Dryad Arbor is changed into another basic land type, it continues to be a green Dryad creature.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a Dryad Arbor gains flash, or you have the ability to play Dryad Arbor as though it had flash (due to Teferi, Mage of Zhalfir or Scout's Warning, for example), you can ignore the normal timing rules for when during your turn you can play a land, but not any other restrictions. You can't play Dryad Arbor during another player's turn, and you can't play Dryad Arbor if you don't have any land plays remaining.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    }
  ],
